WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:06.009
Larry Arnn:
We have with us today an Associate Justice

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:12.940
of the Supreme Court of the United States.
You may read his biography in your program.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:17.810
You will see in it that he was born in Pin
Point, Georgia, south of Savannah, in a shanty

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:23.849
without a bathroom or electricity except light
in the kitchen. You will see that he is a

00:00:23.849 --> 00:00:31.829
proximate descendant of slaves. He was raised
by his grandparents, Myers and Christine Anderson,

00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:37.390
his mother being unable to manage it financially.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:43.030
You must read his book, 'My Grandfather's
Son' which is an inspiration. You will read

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:48.980
that the Justice attended Pius X High School
in Savannah, Georgia from 1962 to '64 ninth

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:55.159
and tenth grades. I will tell you now that
Hillsdale College is proud of its 13th charter

00:00:55.159 --> 00:00:59.909
schools, and that one of those in Savannah,
Georgia operates in that building where Justice

00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:05.890
Thomas went for two years. The eighth grade
of that school is here with us today, along

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:11.329
with their grade headmaster, Ben Payne and
several members of their staff. One of them,

00:01:11.329 --> 00:01:16.100
Hillsdale graduate, Kayla Fletcher, formerly
Cash.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:21.259
Mr. Payne has forbidden me to ask these students
to stand for reasons that may be good, but

00:01:21.259 --> 00:01:25.090
it turns out it's necessary for me to ask
them to stand because yesterday, almost all

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:31.530
of them committed to attend to Hillsdale College,
and I want the admissions office to see who

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:55.100
they are. Stand up, Savannah Classical wherever
you are. (Applause) Justice Thomas was a brilliant

00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:01.789
student, but these guys might be better. I've
been privileged to know the Justice since

00:02:01.789 --> 00:02:09.800
1987. I formed to view early that he is the
greatest public servant I know. When I became

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.940
president of the college, Chairman Brodbeck
asked me who I'd like to speak at my inauguration

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:21.000
ceremonies, and I replied that I of course
hoped it would be this greatest man, and he

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:27.660
did that for me, and I'm grateful to him,
honored beyond any telling, honored beyond

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:31.720
any telling his being here today.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:38.640
There are two reasons I believe of him as
I do. The first is the quality of his work.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.720
I took to redeem to his opinions after he
got on the court. I had been reading everything

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:51.650
he wrote before that. One finds in them a
great act of recovering.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:56.870
He reads the constitution both originally
and profoundly. He sees in it the structure

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:03.540
that provides the form in which the American
people can pursue their purposes. He understands

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.750
that it is their document that they passed
it, and that is the only law that they have

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:16.980
ever passed. He sees that it must remain constant
if it is to remain their own. He says that

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:22.100
if you read it intelligently, you must, you
have the duty to get the rights and the justice

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:29.650
of each case right according to the details,
but also you must understand its large purposes

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:37.010
which culminate I will say according to the
people who wrote it in the purposes I named

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:44.130
as the purposes of this college, that is, that
each one of us may govern himself and his

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:51.750
country so as to live a fully human life.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:57.990
There is in these opinions a beautiful history
of American law and a treasure of civilization

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:03.350
and freedom. These are large claims that I
do not have time to prove them, but if you

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:11.910
write me a letter, I will send you the opinions
that do prove them. The second is his character.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:17.759
He is a man deeply moved by his loves, and
they are the right loves. He loves freedom

00:04:17.759 --> 00:04:19.989
and equality.

00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:25.270
He finds in them what the best Americans have
found, the challenge to live well, to face

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:31.270
one's fears, to build one's character, to
develop one's mind. I have seen him demonstrate

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:39.430
this on so many occasions I cannot count,
and I stand in awe of them. He is I think

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:45.409
the kind of man as the greatest visitor to
our college in the 19th century. I mean, the

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:52.400
runaway slave, Frederick Douglass. That man
like our speaker today was mistreated and

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:57.050
abused because of the color of his skin. Of
course, Douglass more severely than Thomas,

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:01.509
but not more severely than Thomas' ancestors.

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:05.490
The reaction of Douglass to this abuse was
to love the principles and institutions of

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:11.789
our country which condemn the abuse that he
received, because they proclaimed the wrong

00:05:11.789 --> 00:05:20.169
of that abuse and because they offer the hope
that no one may suffer that abuse. The response

00:05:20.169 --> 00:05:27.969
of Justice Thomas to harm is to preach good,
good for all, good for all the same. It is

00:05:27.969 --> 00:05:35.270
the attitude of the great-souled man of the
magnanimous man, and it shows itself all the

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:42.699
time. Seeking to honor this man which we all
come here to do today, of course, it is rather

00:05:42.699 --> 00:05:56.650
we who are honored. Please welcome, Associate
Justice of the Supreme Court, Clarence Thomas.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:04.069
(Applause)

00:06:04.069 --> 00:06:20.029
Clarence Thomas:
Thank you, Dr. Arnn. Thank you all. Thank

00:06:20.029 --> 00:06:23.949
you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you all.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:36.659
Dr. Arnn, Chairman Brodbeck, members of the
board, members of the faculty, family, friends,

00:06:36.659 --> 00:06:45.840
most of all, graduates, I'm honored to be
here. I'm particularly honored because my

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:53.819
bride is with me. We spent quite a bit of
time together, and we like I think to have

00:06:53.819 --> 00:07:00.460
memories together, and this is indeed is a
memory. Again, I'd like to thank Dr. Arnn

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:07.650
for inviting me. Again, I express my deep
honor and gratitude to participate in these

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.680
commencement exercises.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:16.639
It's been quite some years since Virginia
and I have been here together. We've been

00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:25.639
here on separate occasions but rarely together.
Of course, we have known Dr. Larry and Mrs.

00:07:25.639 --> 00:07:32.759
Arnn, Penny Arnn for many, many years as Dr.
Arnn has indicated, and we have been quite

00:07:32.759 --> 00:07:40.419
close to Hillsdale throughout its tenure.
We both admire the work that is being done

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:49.479
here to educate young men and young women.
I was fortunate to have had David Morrell,

00:07:49.479 --> 00:07:53.259
a Hillsdale graduate, clerk for me a few years
back.

00:07:53.259 --> 00:08:01.789
He was an outstanding law clerk and a wonderful,
brilliant, young man. He's also one of my

00:08:01.789 --> 00:08:11.629
daily mass companions. I also had a chance
last evening as Dr. Arnn mentioned to visit

00:08:11.629 --> 00:08:17.069
with the young students who attend Savannah
Classical Academy in my hometown, Savannah,

00:08:17.069 --> 00:08:23.930
Georgia. What a wonderful idea. As Dr. Arnn
indicated, this is the very same school that

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:30.389
I attended high school in in the 1960's.

00:08:30.389 --> 00:08:37.820
This has been a most difficult term at the
court. This difficulty is underscored by the

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:45.160
sudden and tragic passing of my colleague
and friend, Justice Antonin Scalia. I think

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:52.310
it is fitting to say a few words about him,
particularly here. Many will focus on his

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:59.970
intellect and his legal prowess. I do not
demure in either case, but there is so much

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:04.230
more to the man than that.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:11.010
When I think of Justice Scalia, I think of
the good man whom I could instinctively trust

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:18.520
during my first days on the court, and those
were challenging days. He was in the tradition

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:29.320
of the south of my youth, a man of his word,
a man of character. Over the almost 25 years

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:44.850
that we were together, I think we made the
court a better place for each other. (Applause)

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:51.310
I certainly know that he made it a better
place for me. He was kind to me when it mattered

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:59.760
most in those early days. He is and will be
sorely missed.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:06.450
As the year since I attended college etched
toward a half century, I feel a bit out of

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:16.770
place talking with college students or recent
graduates. Much has changed since I left college

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:28.520
in 1971. Things that were once considered
firm have long since lost their vitality,

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:35.710
and much that seemed inconceivable is now
firmly or universally established. Hallmarks

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:46.060
of my youth such as patriotism and religion
seem more like outliers, if not afterthoughts.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:55.140
In a sense, I feel willfully out of place
doing this or any commencement. My words will

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:05.790
perhaps be more of a vintage nature than current
in content.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:16.560
Words actually matter, not a current newspeak.
I admit to be unapologetically Catholic, unapologetically

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:36.230
patriotic, and unapologetically a constitutionalist.
(Applause) In my youth, we had a small farm.

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:44.720
I am convinced that the time I spent there
had much to do with my firm resolve to never

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:54.930
farm again. Work seemed to spring eternal
like the weeds that consumed so much of our

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:59.820
time in our lives and our efforts.

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:06.790
One of the constantly conveyed messages was
our obligation to take care of the land and

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:15.220
to use it to produce food for ourselves and
for others. If there was to be independence,

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:24.640
self-sufficiency or freedom, then we had to
first understand, accept, and then discharge

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:32.750
our responsibilities. The latter were the
necessary but not always sufficient antecedents

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:41.830
or precursors of the former. The only guarantee
was that if you did not discharge your responsibilities,

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:54.090
there could be no independence, no self-sufficiency,
no freedom, no crops. In a broader context,

00:12:54.090 --> 00:13:00.230
we were obligated in our neighborhood to be
good neighbors so that the neighborhood would

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:02.230
thrive.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:07.890
Whether there was to be a clean, thriving
neighborhood was directly connected to our

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:18.160
efforts and to our conduct, so there was always
to our way of thinking a connection and a

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:26.950
relationship between the things we valued
most and our personal obligations or efforts.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:32.550
There could be no freedom without each of
us discharging our responsibilities. That

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:43.250
was first and foremost. In that context, when
we heard the words, 'Duty', 'Honor', 'County',

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:53.310
no more needed to be said, but that is a bygone
era. Today, we rarely hear of our personal

00:13:53.310 --> 00:14:03.130
responsibilities in discussions of broad notions
such as freedom or liberty.

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:11.040
It is as though freedom and liberty exist,
wholly independent of anything we do. Seemingly,

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:20.530
it is our version of predestination or as
my grandfather often warned us or told us

00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:29.700
"Money didn't grow on trees". Perhaps, we
think liberty grows on trees. Their existence

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:40.530
ab initium and continuing
is independent of our conduct. In fact, this

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:47.290
era is one in which any difference or different
treatment is inherently suspect.

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:55.220
Apparently, we all deserve the same reward,
the same status, not withstanding the differences

00:14:55.220 --> 00:15:02.700
in our efforts or our abilities. It is no
wonder then that we hear so often what is

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:12.140
deserved or to what one is entitled. I guess
by this reasoning, the students who took full

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:19.710
advantage of all the spring break bacchanalia
is apparently entitled to the same success

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:27.620
as the conscientious disciplined classmate
who worked and studied while he played. Perhaps

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:34.670
we should redistribute the conscientious student's
grades to make the frolicking classmate his

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:42.730
or her equal. I'm sure the top ten students
would love that.

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:49.280
This leads me to wonder if the same sense
of entitlement applies to that which makes

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:57.440
it possible for us to live in a free country.
After the constitutional convention in Philadelphia,

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:04.970
Benjamin Franklin remarked when asked what
they had done that they had given us a republic

00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:13.120
if we can keep it. Nearly a century later,
in his two-minute speech at Gettysburg, President

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:21.130
Lincoln again spoke of what was required of
us after the battle of Gettysburg. He said,

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:29.170
"It is rather for us to be here dedicated
to the great task remaining before us, that

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:35.730
from these honored dead, we take increased
devotion to that cause for which they gave

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:43.860
the last full measure that we here highly
resolve that these dead shall not have died

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:53.350
in vain, that this nation under God shall
have a new birth of freedom and that government

00:16:53.350 --> 00:17:01.500
of the people, by the people, for the people
shall not perish from the earth." Many who

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:06.370
have gone before us have done precisely that.

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:13.049
They have been dedicated to preserving and
enhancing our nation and the liberties upon

00:17:13.049 --> 00:17:20.919
which it is built in war and in peace. They
have made sure that those who gave the last

00:17:20.919 --> 00:17:29.360
full measure did not do so in vain. Because
you all are graduates of Hillsdale College,

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:36.559
it is quite appropriate and quite convenient
to reflect briefly on their understanding

00:17:36.559 --> 00:17:48.149
of what was to be preserved, what to be earned.
The founders in many success of generations

00:17:48.149 --> 00:17:55.840
believed in natural rights, and that as the
declaration of independence makes clear, to

00:17:55.840 --> 00:18:03.580
establish a government by consent, they gave
up only those rights necessary to create a

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:10.559
limited government. They then structured that
government so that it could not jeopardize

00:18:10.559 --> 00:18:15.999
the liberty that float from these inherent
or natural rights.

00:18:15.999 --> 00:18:21.740
Of course, these limitations have roots that
goes far back as the original Magna Carta

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:28.990
over 800 years ago. Even though this liberty
is inherent, it is neither guaranteed, nor

00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:36.049
short. The very founding documents of our
country for example are an assertion of this

00:18:36.049 --> 00:18:43.509
liberty against arguably the most powerful
man in the world, and it was secured at the

00:18:43.509 --> 00:18:53.460
risk of the lives, fortunes, and sacred honor
of those who dare to assert that liberty.

00:18:53.460 --> 00:19:03.970
Over the lifespan of our great country, occasions
have arisen that require this liberty, as

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:12.129
well as the form of government that ensures
it the defended if it was to survive. At the

00:19:12.129 --> 00:19:19.149
risk of understating what is necessary to
preserve liberty and our form of government,

00:19:19.149 --> 00:19:25.490
I think more and more that it depends on good
citizens discharging their daily duties and

00:19:25.490 --> 00:19:27.480
their daily obligations.

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:37.340
I resist what seems to be somewhat formulaic
or standard fare at commencement exercises,

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:43.669
some broad complaint about societal injustice
and at least one exhortation to the young

00:19:43.669 --> 00:19:52.179
graduates to go out and solve the stated problem
or otherwise to change the world. Having been

00:19:52.179 --> 00:20:00.600
where you all are, I think it is hard enough
to first solve your own problems, not to mention

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:13.909
those problems that often seem to defy solution.
In addressing your own obligations and responsibilities

00:20:13.909 --> 00:20:21.840
in the right way, you actually help to ensure
our liberties and our form of government.

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:30.549
Throughout my youth, even as the contradictions
of segregation persisted, we revered the ideals

00:20:30.549 --> 00:20:37.840
of our great nation. Of course we knew that
our country was like all human institutions

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:47.559
a flawed nation, but we also knew that in
the ideal of liberty lay our last best hope.

00:20:47.559 --> 00:20:54.149
I watched with anguish as so many of the older
people in my life groped and stumbled through

00:20:54.149 --> 00:21:03.929
the darkness of illiteracy or bare literacy.
Yet, they desperately wanted to know to learn.

00:21:03.929 --> 00:21:09.710
They implicitly knew how important it was
to enjoy the fullness of citizenship of this

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:17.350
great country. They had spent an aggregation
of lifetimes standing on the edge of that

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:25.049
dual citizenship that is at the heart of the
14th Amendment of our constitution. Even during

00:21:25.049 --> 00:21:31.970
the last World War, they were willing to fight
for the right to die on foreign soil to defend

00:21:31.970 --> 00:21:40.629
their country even as their patriotic affections
went unreciprocated or unrequited.

00:21:40.629 --> 00:21:46.730
They returned from that horrific war with
dignity to face the indignity of discrimination

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:54.960
at home, yet, the desire to push our nation
to live up to its stated ideals persisted.

00:21:54.960 --> 00:22:02.710
I often wondered why my grandparents remained
such model citizens even when our country's

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:10.649
failures were so obvious. In the arrogance
of my early adult life, I challenged my grandfather

00:22:10.649 --> 00:22:20.429
and doubted the ideals of our nation. He bluntly
asked, "So, where else would you live?" Though

00:22:20.429 --> 00:22:27.980
not a lettered man, he knew that though not
nearly perfect, our constitutional ideals

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:36.460
were perfectable if we worked to protect them
rather than to undermine them.

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:45.700
As he said, "Son, don't throw the baby out
with the bath water." That is don't discard

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:54.990
that which is precious along with that which
is tainted. Sadly, today, when it seems that

00:22:54.990 --> 00:23:02.710
grievances rather than personal conduct are
the means of elevation, this may sound odd

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:14.309
or at least discordant, but those around us
back then seemed to have resolved to conduct

00:23:14.309 --> 00:23:21.210
themselves consistent with the duties that
the ideals of our country demanded. They were

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:29.470
law-abiding, hard-working, disciplined. They
discharged their responsibilities to their

00:23:29.470 --> 00:23:34.350
families and neighbors as best they could.

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:42.980
We were taught that despite unfair treatment,
we were to be good citizens and good people.

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:51.489
If we were to have a functioning neighborhood,
then we had to first be good neighbors. If

00:23:51.489 --> 00:24:00.860
we were to have a good city, state, and country,
we had to first be good citizens. The same

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:08.489
went for our school and our church. The corporal
works of mercy, the greatest commandment,

00:24:08.489 --> 00:24:17.950
"Love thy neighbor as thyself". Just because
someone else wronged us did not justify reciprocal

00:24:17.950 --> 00:24:27.330
conduct on our part. Right was right, and
two wrongs did not make a right.

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:35.549
As my grandfather often said, "We were duty-bound
to do the right thing to do unto others as

00:24:35.549 --> 00:24:43.480
we would have them do unto us". In a sense,
they were teaching us that what we wanted

00:24:43.480 --> 00:24:53.330
to do did not define what was right, nor I
might add, did our capacious litany of wants

00:24:53.330 --> 00:25:03.909
define liberty, rather, what was right defined
what we were required to do and what we were

00:25:03.909 --> 00:25:12.669
permitted to do. It defined our duties and
our responsibilities. Whether those duties

00:25:12.669 --> 00:25:21.249
meant cutting our neighbor's lawn, visiting
the sick, feeding the hungry, or in rare cases,

00:25:21.249 --> 00:25:30.210
going off to war as my brother did, we were
to honorably discharge them. Shortly before

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:38.549
his death in 1983, I sought my grandfather's
advice about how to whether the first wave

00:25:38.549 --> 00:25:43.379
of criticism directly toward me.

00:25:43.379 --> 00:25:51.799
I admit to having been somewhat unnerved back
then by the torrent of negativity. His immediate

00:25:51.799 --> 00:26:00.669
response was simple. "Son, you have to stand
up for what you believe in." To him, that

00:26:00.669 --> 00:26:09.850
was my obligation and my duty. Perhaps, it
is at times like that when you lack both strength

00:26:09.850 --> 00:26:18.909
and courage that the clarity of our obligation
supplies both. Duty, honor, country. The clarity

00:26:18.909 --> 00:26:21.960
of obligation.

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:31.989
As I admitted at the outset, I am of a different
time. I knew no one for example who was surprised

00:26:31.989 --> 00:26:41.649
when President John F. Kennedy famously said
at his inauguration in 1961, "Ask not what

00:26:41.649 --> 00:26:48.330
your country can do for you, but what you
can do for your country." That sentiment was

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:54.879
as common as saying The Pledge of Allegiance
and singing 'The Star-Spangled Banner' and

00:26:54.879 --> 00:27:03.960
as pervasive as shopping at Army Navy Surplus
stores. Today of course, there is much more

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:11.009
focus on our rights as citizens and what we
are owed. It is not often that one hears of

00:27:11.009 --> 00:27:19.039
our obligations or our duties as citizens,
unless of course there's talk about duty to

00:27:19.039 --> 00:27:26.009
submit to yet another new policy being suggested
or proposed.

00:27:26.009 --> 00:27:33.190
My grandfather often said that "If we didn't
work, we didn't eat, or if we didn't plant,

00:27:33.190 --> 00:27:40.009
we didn't harvest". There was to always be
a relationship as I said earlier between our

00:27:40.009 --> 00:27:49.379
responsibilities and our benefits. In agrarian
societies, that is more obvious. As society

00:27:49.379 --> 00:27:58.289
becomes more complex and specialized, this
is more difficult to discern. Let's look at

00:27:58.289 --> 00:28:00.759
this a different way.

00:28:00.759 --> 00:28:06.889
If you continue to run up charges on your
credit card and never make a payment, at some

00:28:06.889 --> 00:28:13.509
point, you reach your credit limit. If you
continue to make withdrawals from your savings

00:28:13.509 --> 00:28:21.369
account and make no deposits, eventually,
you deplete your funds. Why is it not the

00:28:21.369 --> 00:28:29.929
case then that if we continue to consume the
benefits of a free society without replenishing

00:28:29.929 --> 00:28:37.720
or nourishing it, we will eventually deplete
it? If we are not making deposits to deplenish

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:47.200
our liberties, then who is? Are we content
to let others do the work, to let a few give

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:56.830
the last full measure for liberty while we
consume the benefits? If so, perhaps one day,

00:28:56.830 --> 00:29:03.879
we will run out of other people's sacrifice
and courage, and perhaps we will run out of

00:29:03.879 --> 00:29:13.960
courageous people wiling to make the sacrifice,
but this is Hillsdale College, and you are

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:19.100
special, that shining city on a hill.

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:26.169
Hillsdale is a trusty of the heritage that
finds us clear expression in the American

00:29:26.169 --> 00:29:36.369
experiment of self-government under law. The
very existence of Hillsdale connotes independence.

00:29:36.369 --> 00:29:43.230
It understands that liberty is an antecedent
of government, not a benefit from government.

00:29:43.230 --> 00:29:51.859
I offer you, graduates a few brief suggestions
to make your contributions to liberty, your

00:29:51.859 --> 00:29:59.549
deposits to the account of liberty. Today
is just the end of the beginning of your young

00:29:59.549 --> 00:30:08.720
lives, and it is the beginning, the commencement
of the rest of your lives, and hopefully their

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:13.090
long, fruitful lives and a free country.

00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:19.980
There is much more to come, and it will not
be with the guiding hands of your parents.

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:32.570
Perhaps your hand will be required to guide
them. Indeed, some of you won't most assuredly

00:30:32.570 --> 00:30:38.619
be called upon to do the very hard things
to preserve liberty, perhaps even given the

00:30:38.619 --> 00:30:46.749
last full measure, but all of you will be
called upon to provide that firm foundation

00:30:46.749 --> 00:30:55.190
of citizenship by carrying out your obligations
in much the way that those around you did

00:30:55.190 --> 00:31:02.479
and so many did during my youth. You are to
be the example to others that they were to

00:31:02.479 --> 00:31:11.789
you. The greatest lecture or sermon you will
give is your example.

00:31:11.789 --> 00:31:20.019
What you do will matter far more than what
you say. As the years have swiftly moved by,

00:31:20.019 --> 00:31:28.869
I have often reflected on the important citizenship
lessons of my life. For the most part, it

00:31:28.869 --> 00:31:39.190
was the unplanned array of small things. There
was the kind gesture from the neighbor. It

00:31:39.190 --> 00:31:47.330
was my grandmother dividing our dinner because
another person showed up unannounced. It was

00:31:47.330 --> 00:31:55.320
the strangers stopping to help us get our
crops out of the field before a big storm.

00:31:55.320 --> 00:32:02.359
There were the Irish nuns who believed in
us and lived in our neighborhood. There was

00:32:02.359 --> 00:32:08.700
the librarian who brought books to mass so
that I would not be without reading materials

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:18.879
on the farm. Small lessons such as these became
big lessons for how to live our lives. We

00:32:18.879 --> 00:32:27.929
watched and learned what it means to be a
good person, a good neighbor, or a good citizen.

00:32:27.929 --> 00:32:36.940
Who will be watching you, and what will you
be teaching them? After this commencement,

00:32:36.940 --> 00:32:43.570
I implore you to take a few minutes to thank
those who made it possible for you to come

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:51.649
this far, your parents, your teachers, your
pastor, your coaches. You know who helped

00:32:51.649 --> 00:32:53.049
you.

00:32:53.049 --> 00:32:59.609
Take a few minutes to show your gratitude.
These are the people who have shown you how

00:32:59.609 --> 00:33:10.169
to sacrifice for those whom they love even
when that sacrifice is not always appreciated.

00:33:10.169 --> 00:33:17.850
As you go through life, try to be that person
whose actions teach others how to be better

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:27.289
people and better citizens. Reach out to that
shy person whose not so popular. Stand up

00:33:27.289 --> 00:33:35.220
for others when they're being treated unfairly
and small things and large. Take the time

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:41.609
to listen to that friend who's having a difficult
time.

00:33:41.609 --> 00:33:49.369
Do not hide your faith and your beliefs under
a bushel basket especially in this world that

00:33:49.369 --> 00:33:58.450
seems to have gone mad with political correctness.
Treat others the way you would like to be

00:33:58.450 --> 00:34:08.790
treated if you stood in their shoes. These
small lessons become the unplanned syllabus

00:34:08.790 --> 00:34:17.049
for becoming a good citizen, and your efforts
to live them will help to form the fabric

00:34:17.049 --> 00:34:24.799
of a civil society and a free and prosperous
nation where inherent equality and liberty

00:34:24.799 --> 00:34:34.669
are inviolable. You are men and women of Hillsdale
College, a school that has stood fast on its

00:34:34.669 --> 00:34:44.849
principles and its traditions at great sacrifice
and great cost. You are men and women of Hillsdale's

00:34:44.849 --> 00:34:51.589
steeped in the best traditions and principles
of our great nation.

00:34:51.589 --> 00:34:59.190
If you don't lead by example, who will? I
have ever faith that you will be the beacon

00:34:59.190 --> 00:35:07.410
of light for others to follow, that city on
a hill that cannot be hidden. May God bless

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:13.700
each of you now and throughout your lives
and may God bless America. Thank you. (Applause)

